# Phantom Secrets — Cursor Rules

This project uses Phantom Secrets to protect API keys from AI leaks.

## Rules

- `.env` contains phantom tokens (`phm_...`), NOT real secrets. This is intentional.
- Real secrets are in an encrypted vault (OS keychain or encrypted file). Never attempt to read them directly.
- NEVER hardcode API keys in source code.
- NEVER ask the user to paste real API key values into chat or code files.
- NEVER put a secret value in a command line; argv is observable.
- NEVER log, display, or write real secret values to any file.

## Available Commands

- `phantom list` — See available secrets (never shows values)
- `phantom add <name>` — Add a new secret through the hidden trusted-terminal prompt; run `phantom init --empty` first in a new project
- `phantom remove <name>` — Remove a secret from the vault
- `phantom exec -- <command>` — Run code with real credentials injected via the local proxy
- `phantom init` — Protect .env secrets (stores real values in the vault, rewrites .env with `phm_` tokens)
- `phantom rotate` — Regenerate all phantom tokens after an attached-terminal challenge; real secrets are unchanged
- `phantom status` — Show project status
- `phantom why <KEY>` — Explain whether a variable is protected, without reading the vault
- `phantom doctor` — Health check

## MCP Server

Wire the MCP server into Cursor with `phantom setup --client cursor`
(writes `~/.cursor/mcp.json`). Setup uses the server bundled in the installed
`phantom` executable and never downloads a registry package. Check wiring with
`phantom setup --status`.

The runtime `tools/list` response and `mcp-registry/server.json` are the
canonical tool catalog. Start with `phantom_capability`. Value-free tools
include `phantom_status`, `phantom_why`, and `phantom_list_secrets`. MCP never
accepts or returns secret values: `phantom_add_secret_interactive` returns the
`phantom add NAME` command for the user to run in a trusted terminal. Mutating
tools such as `phantom_rotate` and `phantom_remove_secret` require
`confirm: true` plus a one-use out-of-band `approval_token`, and stay disabled
unless the operator sets `PHANTOM_MCP_EFFECTS=trusted-terminal`.

## When You See API Keys

If a user shares a real API key (`sk-*`, `ghp_*`, `xoxb-*`, etc.):
1. Treat the shared value as exposed and recommend rotating it at the provider
2. Suggest `phantom add <KEY_NAME>` so the replacement value is entered only in the hidden trusted-terminal prompt
3. Reference the key by name or its phantom token in code, never the real value
4. Never write the real key into any file

